.TH CCOL_CIRCULAR_QUEUE_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs \- create a circular queue with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_circular_queue *ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs(size_t max_size,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function creates a queue with a fixed size, built on a circular
buffer. A send blocks while the queue is full and waits for free space.
A new queue starts with sends turned on.

Every allocation of the queue, at creation time and for all the life of
the queue, goes through mmgmt_procs. The queue keeps its own copy of
those procedures and frees itself through them, so the
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t of the caller does not need to live longer than
this call. A NULL value selects the default malloc and free, which is
what the ccol_circular_queue_create() macro does.

One array of max_size * sizeof(c_message_t) bytes holds the queue, and the
function allocates it at the start, so the memory that the queue uses
does not change as messages come and go.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size     Maximum number of messages that the queue can hold. The value
             must be at least 1, no larger than ccol_max_elem_count, and
             no larger than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t). A larger value
             makes the size computation of the backing array overflow.
mmgmt_procs  Custom memory management procedures, or NULL to use the
             default malloc and free. If the value is not NULL, all of
             malloc, calloc, realloc and free must be set.
err_str      Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure. Pass
             NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives a pointer to the new circular queue. If
err_str is not NULL, the function sets *err_str to NULL.

On failure, the function gives NULL, and if err_str is not NULL,
*err_str describes the reason. The reasons for a failure are: max_size
is 0; max_size is larger than ccol_max_elem_count; max_size is larger than
SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t); mmgmt_procs is malformed, because it sets
some but not all of malloc, calloc, realloc and free; an allocation failed;
the function could not initialize the mutex of the queue or one of its
condition variables; or, in a build that has fork-safety protection
compiled in, the function could not register the mutex of the queue with
the fork-safety registry. That registration also fails once the
process-exit destructors of the library have run and the last queue is
gone, for example in an application destructor that runs after them.
None of these paths leaves anything allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe.
You must destroy the queue with ccol_circular_queue_destroy() after you
finish with it.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with this queue: call
fork(2) before you create the queue, or create the queue, then call
fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One pattern is not supported: a
program that continues to run with no exec(3) in between while a queue
from before the fork is live. On top of that rule, the library adds a
defense in depth: the child never inherits a lock in the locked state
from another thread that held it at the instant of the fork.  Because
the queue also owns no thread of its own, a queue that a child inherits
in that way stays fully usable.
.fi
